  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1147 AM MDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nWith high pressure building in from the west, we'll see a pattern \nshift with much drier weather than we've had for the last few weeks. \nHere's the bottom line up front for the hay farmers and ranchers, \nnow is probably the best time for the last hay cutting. Days are \ngetting shorter, temperatures are getting cooler, and the Fall is \nnot known for long dry spells. That being said, Daytime high \ntemperatures look to run about five degrees above normal under \nmostly sunny skies with afternoon relative humidity running 25% in \nthe lower valleys to 35% in the higher valleys and over the higher \nterrain. With the moisture in the ground, will likely see dew each \nmorning. A disturbance early next week and a second later in the \nweek bring chances for showers mostly to the southern mountains, but \nthe higher valleys could see a slight chance for light showers up \nnorth along the I-70 corridor. \n\nNow for the synoptic view, high pressure building in to the Four \nCorners area will pull monsoonal moisture north into the Great Basin \nthrough the weekend. With the residual moisture in the area, there \nis a slight chance for a shower or thunderstorms over the San Juans \nSunday and Monday afternoon. Models are in good agreement with a \nshortwave system moving in off the Pacific Monday that will pick up \nthe moisture in the Great Basin bringing 22-35% chances for showers \nand thunderstorms to the Colorado mountains Tuesday. Models are \nhinting that this pattern will repeat again next weekend with \nanother shortwave moving in off the Pacific, but it is a long ways \nout and there is less agreement on the timing and track. FOr the \nmost part, the weather looks to be pretty nice for the next week or \nso, so get out and enjoy it along with the Fall colors.",
